\begin_layout Section
Spheres
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "sec:spheres"

\end_inset

 In this section we present our 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+\eps)$
\end_inset

-approximation algorithm for the case of 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional spheres.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\C=\{C_{1},C_{2},\dots,C_{n}\}$
\end_inset

 be a set of 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 number of 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional hyperspheres.
 We denote the radius and profit of each hypersphere 
\begin_inset Formula $S_{i}\in\calS$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\profit_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 For an object 
\begin_inset Formula $C$
\end_inset

 we denote its volume (or area in 2-dimension) to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\area(C)$
\end_inset

.
 For a collection of objects 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{A}$
\end_inset

, we define its volume and profit to be 
\begin_inset Formula $\area(\mathcal{A}):=\sum_{C_{i}\in A}\area(C_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\profit(\mathcal{A}):=\sum_{C_{i}\in A}\profit(C_{i})$
\end_inset

, respectively.
 We are given a unit knapsack 
\begin_inset Formula $K:=[0,1]^{d}$
\end_inset

.
 
