\emph on
(
\begin_inset Formula $\star$
\end_inset

)
\emph default
 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "big_sol"

\end_inset

 Any feasible solution can contain at most 
\begin_inset Formula $(1/\eps)^{O_{k}(1)}$
\end_inset

 large circles.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{restatable}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We guess the a feasible solution of the large circles in 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

 that satisfy the packing constraints in time 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{(1/\eps)^{O_{k}(1)}}$
\end_inset

, denote them by 
\begin_inset Formula $\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 In related problems, like the two-dimensional knapsack problem with squares
 or rectangles, one can easily guess the correct placement of the guessed
 large circles.
 (assuming rational input data).
 For packing circles, it is not clear that there if there is a packing in
 which the centers of the circles are placed at rational coordinates.
 However, if we pack polygons as in the next section, we can guarentee that
 there is an optimal solution in which the corner of each polygon has a
 rational coordinate.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, instead we first guess for each circle 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 an 
\emph on
estimate 
\emph default
for its placement in 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

.
 Denote by 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}^{(1)},\hat{x}_{i}^{(2)}\in[0,1]$
\end_inset

 the coordinates of the center of 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}$
\end_inset

 in 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

.
 We guess values 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)}\in\{0,\frac{\eps}{n},\frac{2\eps}{n},...,1\}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}^{(1)}\in[\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)}+\frac{\eps}{n})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}^{(2)}\in[\tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)},\tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)}+\frac{\eps}{n})$
\end_inset

.
 Note that there are only 
\begin_inset Formula $O(n^{2}/\eps^{2})$
\end_inset

 possibilities for each 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

, and hence only 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{(1/\eps)^{O_{k}(1)}}$
\end_inset

 possibilities overall for all circles 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given these guessed values 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

 for each circle 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

, we verify that our guess was correct or not, i.e., confirm that there exists,
 indeed a corresponding placement for each circle 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 such that the circles in 
\begin_inset Formula $\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 do not overlap.
 Therefore, we define a system of quadratic inequalities that describe the
 problem of finding such a placement.
 We require that this placement is consistent with our guesses 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{alignat}{2}
\max\{\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)},r_{i}\}\leqslant x_{i}^{(1)} & \leqslant\min\left\{ \tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)}+\frac{\eps}{n},1-r_{i}\right\}  & \,\,\,\,\, & \forall C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}\nonumber \\
\max\{\tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)},r_{i}\}\leqslant x_{i}^{(2)} & \leqslant\min\left\{ \tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)}+\frac{\eps}{n},1-r_{i}\right\}  &  & \forall C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}\label{eq:equations}\\
(x_{i}^{(1)}-x_{j}^{(1)})^{2}+(x_{i}^{(2)}-x_{j}^{(2)})^{2} & \geqslant(r_{i}+r_{j})^{2} &  & \forall C_{i},C_{j}\in\C_{L}^{*}\nonumber \\
x_{i}^{(1)},x_{i}^{(2)} & \geqslant0 &  & \forall C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}\nonumber 
\end{alignat}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $|\C_{L}^{*}|=:t$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the above system has 
\begin_inset Formula $2t$
\end_inset

 variables and 
\begin_inset Formula $k:=O(t^{2})$
\end_inset

 constraints.
 It is not clear how to compute a solution to this system in polynomial
 time.
 It is not even clear that it has a solution in which each variable has
 a rational value.
 However, in polynomial time, we can 
\emph on
decide
\emph default
 whether it has a solution (without computing the solution itself) using
 an algorithm from 
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "GrigorevVV88"
literal "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that the set of solutions satisfying system (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:equations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) is a semi-algebraic set in the field of real numbers.
 Thus, whether a given set of circles can be packed or not (the decision
 problem) reduces to a decision problem of whether this semi-algebraic set
 is nonempty or not.
 Here, each constraint 
\begin_inset Formula $i\in[k]$
\end_inset

 in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:equations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

) can be written as a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}(x_{1}^{(1)},x_{1}^{(2)},\dots,x_{t}^{(1)},x_{t}^{(2)})\geqslant0$
\end_inset

 where each 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{i}\in\mathbb{Q}[x_{1}^{(1)},x_{1}^{(2)},\dots,x_{t}^{(1)},x_{t}^{(2)}]$
\end_inset

 is a polynomial with rational coefficients of degree at most two.
 Thus deciding the circle packing problem is equivalent to deciding the
 truth of the following formula: 
\begin_inset Formula $F:=(\exists x_{1}^{(1)})(\exists x_{1}^{(2)})\dots(\exists x_{t}^{(1)})(\exists x_{t}^{(2)})\land_{i=0}^{k}f_{i}(x_{1}^{(1)},x_{1}^{(2)},\dots,x_{t}^{(1)},x_{t}^{(2)})\geqslant0$
\end_inset

.
 To solve this decision problem, we use the following result.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{theorem}[
\backslash
citeGrigorevVV88]
\end_layout

\end_inset

 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:GrigorevVV88"

\end_inset

 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{1},f_{2},\dots,f_{k}\in\mathbb{Q}[x_{1}^{(1)},x_{1}^{(2)},\dots,x_{t}^{(1)},x_{t}^{(2)}]$
\end_inset

 be polynomials with absolute value of any coefficient to be represented
 by 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

 bits and maximum degree 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta$
\end_inset

.
 There is an algorithm that decides whether the formula 
\begin_inset Formula $F:=(\exists x_{1}^{(1)})(\exists x_{1}^{(2)})\dots(\exists x_{t}^{(1)})(\exists x_{t}^{(2)})\land_{i=0}^{k}f_{i}(x_{1},y_{1},\dots,x_{n},y_{n})\geqslant0$
\end_inset

 is true, with a running time of 
\begin_inset Formula $M^{O(1)}(k\Delta)^{O(t^{2})}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If it is true, the algorithm also returns polynomials 
\begin_inset Formula $f,g_{1},h_{1},\dots,g_{t},h_{t}\in\mathbb{Q}[x]$
\end_inset

 with coefficients of bit size at most 
\begin_inset Formula $M^{O(1)}(k\Delta)^{O(t)}$
\end_inset

 and maximum degree 
\begin_inset Formula $k^{O(t)}$
\end_inset

, such that for a root 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

, the assignment 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}^{(1)}=g_{1}(x),x_{1}^{(2)}=h_{1}(x),\dots,x_{t}^{(1)}=g_{n}(x),x_{t}^{(2)}=h_{n}(x)$
\end_inset

 satisfies the formula 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Moreover, for any rational 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha>0$
\end_inset

, it returns values 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{1}^{(1)},\bar{x}_{1}^{(2)}\dots,\bar{x}_{t}^{(1)},\bar{x}_{t}^{(2)}\in\mathbb{Q}$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $|\bar{x}_{i}^{(1)}-{x}_{i}^{(1)}|\leqslant\alpha$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $|\bar{x}always_{i}^{(2)}-{x}_{i}^{(2)}|\leqslant\alpha$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $1\leqslant i\leqslant t$
\end_inset

, in time at most 
\begin_inset Formula $(\log(1/\alpha)M)^{O(1)}(k\Delta)^{O(t^{2})}$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{theorem}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We crucially use here that our system has only constantly many variables
 and constraints, i.e., in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:equations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

), we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta,k,t$
\end_inset

 to be constants and 
\begin_inset Formula $M$
\end_inset

