\begin_layout Subsection
Placing small circles
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:placing_small"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We want to select small circles from 
\begin_inset Formula $\C$
\end_inset

 and place them inside the knapsack, so that they do not overlap with each
 other or with the circles in 
\begin_inset Formula $\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 To this end, we define 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsc:=\epsl^{12}$
\end_inset

 (i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\epss=\epsl^{12}\epsc=\epsc^{2})$
\end_inset

 to subdivide the knapsack into a grid with 
\begin_inset Formula $1/\epsc^{2}$
\end_inset

 square grid cells of side length 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsc$
\end_inset

 .
 Our choice of the parameters ensures that each small circle is small compared
 to each grid cell and each large circle is big compared to each grid cell.
 Formally, for each 
\begin_inset Formula $\ell,\ell'\in\{0,1,\dots,\frac{1}{\epsc}-1\}$
\end_inset

 we define a grid cell 
\begin_inset Formula $G_{\ell,\ell'}:=[\ell\cdot\epsc,(\ell+1)\cdot\epsc)\times[\ell'\cdot\epsc,(\ell'+1)\cdot\epsc)$
\end_inset

.
 We define the set of all grid cells by 
\begin_inset Formula $\G:=\{G_{\ell,\ell'}:\ell,\ell'\in\{0,1,\dots,1/\epsc-1\}\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We say that a placement of a circle 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
legal
\emph default
 if its center is placed at a point 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i}^{(1)},x_{i}^{(2)})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\max\{\tilde{x}_{i}^{(s)},r_{i}\}\leqslant x_{i}^{(s)}\leqslant\min\left\{ \tilde{x}_{i}^{(s)}+\frac{\eps}{n},1-r_{i}\right\} $
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in\{1,2\}$
\end_inset

.
 We show that there is a structured packing with near-optimal profit in
 which each small circle is contained in a grid cell that does not intersect
 with any large circle in 
\begin_inset Formula $\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

 in any legal packing of them.
 This will allow us to decouple the remaining problem for the small circles
 from the large circles, even though we do not know the exact placement
 for the latter.
 Moreover, in each grid cell the small circles use only a reduced area of
 size 
\begin_inset Formula $(1-\eps)\epsc\times(1-\eps)\epsc$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\C_{S}^{*}$
\end_inset

 denote the small circles in 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

