\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}^{(2)}\in[\bar{x}_{i}^{(2)},\bar{x}_{i}^{(2)}+\frac{1}{2^{n/\eps}})$
\end_inset

 for some 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 The reason is that our system of inequalites
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:equations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

 can have solutions that differ from 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{i}^{(1)},\hat{x}_{i}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

 for some (or even all) large circles 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Because of this, we guessed the estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{x}_{i}^{(2)}$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $C_{i}\in\C_{L}^{*}$
\end_inset

, so that we can assume that these estimates correspond to 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

 and not just to some arbitrary solution to 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:equations"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\end_inset

If it were true that there is always a 
\begin_inset Formula $(1+\eps)$
\end_inset

-approximate solution in which the center of each circle has rational coordinate
s that can be encoded with a polynomially bounded number of bits, then we
 could choose 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

 appropriately to compute it.
 More precisely, we could compute a range for each coordinate that contains
 only one rational number with a bounded number of bits, and we could compute
 this number afterward.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Higher dimensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Our techniques from the previous section extend directly to the problem
 of packing hyperspheres in any (constant) dimension 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

.
 In that case, there can be at most 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{\eps,d}(1)$
\end_inset

 large hyperspheres in 
\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

 and, hence, we can guess them in time 
\begin_inset Formula $n^{O_{\eps,d}(1)}$
\end_inset

.
 Like above, for each large hypersphere 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, we can guess estimates 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{x}_{i}^{(1)},...,\tilde{x}_{i}^{(d)}$
\end_inset

 for the coordinates of its center in
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\OPT$
\end_inset

.
 As before, we can argue that there are at least a certain number of white
 grid cells which can compensate for the lost space due to the gray grid
 cells.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
begin{thm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "thm:d-dim-ptas"

\end_inset

 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $d\in\N$
\end_inset

 be a fixed constant.
 For the geometric knapsack problem with 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-dimensional hyperspheres, there is a polynomial time algorithm that computes
 a set of hyperspheres 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\C}$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $p(\tilde{\C})\geqslant(1-\eps)\OPT$
\end_inset

 that can be placed non-overlappingly inside the knapsack.
 For all but 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{\eps}(1)$
\end_inset

 hyperspheres in 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\C}$
\end_inset

 we compute the precise coordinate of the corresponding packing; for the
 other 
\begin_inset Formula $O_{\eps}(1)$
\end_inset

 circles we compute an estimate of the packing with an additive error of
 at most 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2^{n/\eps}}$
\end_inset

 in each dimension.
 
\begin_inset ERT
status collapsed

\begin_layout Plain Layout


\backslash
end{thm}
\end_layout

\end_inset


